#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "522a785c-3e1e-5515-82c9-8980b448bee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10553" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6ad02a19-7b07-5e07-8388-b46fd1cc48ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-10554" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "37af544e-feab-52b0-bcef-887c723093b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10764" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5406e24-5e2c-5e28-a011-615daf235048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317021} {:answer/id #uuid "bfeb4047-6209-56ce-84e8-a28b5b0a8ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317022} {:answer/id #uuid "74eb9c07-4eb1-559d-9d06-eaeb7465ee27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317023} {:answer/id #uuid "18a92478-c825-5593-96d7-1548d8d2ef3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317024} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6b724a-35d2-5e47-b2fa-8ed3898fb26a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74eb9c07-4eb1-559d-9d06-eaeb7465ee27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317023} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8e23acf-7ccf-5757-821c-89c55aed5b9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40f084aa-b80e-58f3-b339-c0f4c81b3533" :question/math-academy-id "q-100378" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/45e8ddd8ddb33dc8d6a701ed75878271b331ce6b3591ba8fae9f4fa49020c12b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1331ccfd-5799-52f0-92c9-42437b8d9bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317028} {:answer/id #uuid "0b108031-4124-57ec-9844-c213d2e4dc23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317029} {:answer/id #uuid "c4952de8-1cd2-5da5-8769-56da18627c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317030} {:answer/id #uuid "7b73fad5-876c-55eb-88bf-4b847cd6e4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317031} {:answer/id #uuid "3114b948-3060-5b59-89f3-827cfe2524a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b108031-4124-57ec-9844-c213d2e4dc23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317029} :answer-field/id #uuid "28565e4b-6627-5b45-893a-e114d620b023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c5d67b1-17eb-5fed-900a-3f60a9deeff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100424" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/4844d6220d04a6a1fffdd5a485a9e6724aa6065397b8c3a009157a7a2dafc3cf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27eb827-5889-5235-9bae-d6c309c0f5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189434} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fe5d8f-33d8-56cb-bf17-65f6defef94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\ln 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189435} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae381a4-c807-564c-bf13-80830481f58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189436} {:answer/id #uuid "201e2ae7-1d3a-541f-a950-972540b81ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189437} {:answer/id #uuid "4d361e7a-1c99-54a6-8471-22aba0b236d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d361e7a-1c99-54a6-8471-22aba0b236d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189438} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0a0de0-58ce-563f-bc1d-7a5688015c1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4a29a66-652c-5a42-830a-8d138db1036c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100494" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{2}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{2}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afac4dd8-6e35-5226-9687-201646535d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317035} {:answer/id #uuid "f47b946b-20d1-5f6d-a285-c47e250e7087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317036} {:answer/id #uuid "224451d0-8b48-5227-ad8c-e1aa730b4898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317037} {:answer/id #uuid "bc20f57b-2a32-54cb-b418-754095cab358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317038} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2e8c06-e722-5c9f-942f-f233f88a1358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d2e8c06-e722-5c9f-942f-f233f88a1358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317039} :answer-field/id #uuid "a065a4c0-893c-5da3-b9da-d4021c032d1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f999842-4b67-56b3-8661-e898a8c57652" :question/math-academy-id "q-152257" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{1-x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{1-x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}+\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/4ef3194e946aec805866202a5e0ad521291d71b1fabc2df68d6cb1a4d93da42b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{1-x}}}{2\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{e}-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3bd3888-4147-5cca-87d6-ef52e7d33408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317042} {:answer/id #uuid "11ba0022-8ee0-5b7b-a189-37ed3554abd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317043} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b039e2-90ed-53ae-80fa-ab83412812cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317044} {:answer/id #uuid "77087391-186d-5d91-931a-5c8ac49a7c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317045} {:answer/id #uuid "f422bd08-9f0c-5578-ab9f-83881b07eecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f422bd08-9f0c-5578-ab9f-83881b07eecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317046} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aacc6ce-36f1-5059-9e7e-0497d1596a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30e19b72-0416-5b80-938a-cf37e67f6c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-152265" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{e}^{u}]_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcd669c5-15c8-58a2-8b64-7ef90c4a3660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" 