f 17592186317054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28679009-a60a-595f-9b07-f8a6c24ee8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317056} {:answer/id #uuid "1f58aaa3-b2dd-5cfc-957f-ada1fd0ae91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317057} {:answer/id #uuid "15d4f6c6-2675-59a6-91b8-ef0c5cb88ee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317058} {:answer/id #uuid "704a1e35-75a1-5a50-a1a5-b206519d97c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317059} {:answer/id #uuid "5e593295-571c-5135-9e5b-8082235affba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28679009-a60a-595f-9b07-f8a6c24ee8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317056} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9a6032-9a7d-5990-bc32-a63ae0286320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdbc143c-1d4b-5ff7-8e96-4b65f8c9923e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152879" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a235cf02-e2f7-5789-b5b0-fa2e4829cb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\ln(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317063} {:answer/id #uuid "30f5688f-488e-576a-a93d-3f7e26d20e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a235cf02-e2f7-5789-b5b0-fa2e4829cb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\ln(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317063} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d050717-040b-54d9-841d-e702ccee418c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e67e48d-05b2-55a2-b68d-67c36a777690" :question/math-academy-id "q-346615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(5-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/83f7d663fa66184b56c4f79d61f6e17012714a3de7512202b3022e91d4325437.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d76b388-31ea-5593-ba50-2fbabd4cccd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d76b388-31ea-5593-ba50-2fbabd4cccd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317067} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5b5429-d1e6-5907-92a4-fbe28584db05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68bb759e-539a-555e-bba1-a116dee326cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-350800" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & =7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}7{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =7(\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6748e9a-bfc9-580a-97b9-b0ca4ee46133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317070} {:answer/id #uuid "f36a0474-bf39-5f62-b1be-5f7138e8a915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6e^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6748e9a-bfc9-580a-97b9-b0ca4ee46133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317070} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c2fc38-cc18-597f-9df5-5b66fb0ac0a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b798f4b7-e5ea-58e3-9525-4ab4622494f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-350809" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/61a2aafc6dadee9e392e098f77839fdd99f3bb5d30c62cf06fef1be08c4b2682.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fe1340-759b-586c-837e-bd624a59f9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06fe1340-759b-586c-837e-bd624a59f9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301345} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f2aa46-8db9-554e-a213-55c6023dfb62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0191a30-c2e2-52a8-94ee-6284596521e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350830" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/ade58ce56684420fb0f00236af50bbb54f76801fc8870e1cce135cbd51f1b726.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa485675-6e54-5354-a5be-95b88c6790a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0aeedd54-c10a-53bd-b367-88f50ec08852" :question/math-academy-id "q-351320" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d182f3-9d75-54c3-b417-f852e1ef78eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d182f3-9d75-54c3-b417-f852e1ef78eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317077} :answer-field/id #uuid "b096aed0-3798-522b-84a3-5f4f8d7e125f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8db43b84-59e1-5d8b-9409-aff2fd00efef" :question/math-academy-id "q-351416" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(8-{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/c2af905a46cea7b7512a727a5e53a2a178e4902f7114f86439365684e6685efa.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264ec97b-82ef-5a8e-b277-97d6e2cf1063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317080} {:answer/id #uuid "1a89b222-3e93-5590-890c-fa1e2418fd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "264ec97b-82ef-5a8e-b277-97d6e2cf1063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317080} :answer-field/id #uuid "c525b007-0a7c-5605-bc48-934795e9ca16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "808f3d8c-e897-5a7c-8a7c-2cad7570d3cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-351529" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{5u}}{5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{5x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/2a24352ca6421b3aa61f3f29e3c19373cca011feafce263775a2f692f67ad36b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
